---
/**
 * The roadmap page.
 *
 * Renders `itemsByPriority()` straight out of `docs/roadmap.json`, so an item
 * that flips from planned to shipped in the repo flips here on the next build.
 * The prose around the items — the selection bar, the sequencing argument and
 * the four premises that did not survive checking — is the material from
 * `docs/ROADMAP.md`, which is also published in full under /docs/roadmap/.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import StatusChip from '../components/StatusChip.astro';
import Scoreboard from '../components/Scoreboard.astro';
import { inlineMarkdown } from '../components/inline';
import { itemsByPriority, items, counts, withBase, adrHref } from '../lib/roadmap';

const groups = itemsByPriority();
const tally = counts(items);

const priorityIntro: Record<number, { label: string; text: string }> = {
  1: {
    label: 'A chain, not a set',
    text:
      'Ingestion first, because lineage and cataloguing are meaningless without something producing traceable ingestion events — you cannot trace provenance for data that arrived by hand. Lineage next, because it is nearly free once ingestion exists: the correlation id is already threaded through every action, and `ash_strangler` already ships an OpenLineage emitter for column-level mappings. The integration hub sits alongside rather than after, because it answers a different question — data you pull versus systems you talk to. Sharing this tier is the composition already half-built, which is first because it is the only item where the claim on the landing page is currently ahead of the code.',
  },
  2: {
    label: 'Consumes what priority 1 produces',
    text:
      'A catalog needs something to catalogue; BI needs something to report on. API versioning sits here rather than lower because it is the item most likely to be needed *before* it is wanted — the first external consumer of the JSON:API surface is the point after which changing it becomes expensive.',
  },
  3: {
    label: 'Genuinely deferrable',
    text:
      'Feature flags matter when there are enough users to stage a rollout to, and an observability backend matters when there is enough traffic for a trace to tell you something a log would not.',
  },
  4: {
    label: 'Genuinely deferrable',
    text:
      'Entity resolution matters when the same customer has arrived from three systems, which is a consequence of priority 1 rather than a precondition for it.',
  },
};

/** The four premises this roadmap was drafted on that turned out to be wrong. */
const corrections: { claim: string; correction: string }[] = [
  {
    claim: 'Meltano is Apache-2.0, and Airbyte’s connectors are MIT.',
    correction:
      'Meltano is **MIT**. And Airbyte’s connectors moved from MIT to ELv2 in July 2025, so the "source-available platform, open connectors" split no longer holds — the fallback is source-available throughout.',
  },
  {
    claim: 'The ingestion tool will emit lineage events.',
    correction:
      'Neither Meltano nor Airbyte emits OpenLineage. That leaves a hole in the lineage graph exactly where external data enters. ADR 0008’s own argument applies — a diagram that omits edges it could not work out is worse than no diagram, because a reader cannot tell "nothing feeds this" from "the generator gave up" — so the hole is drawn as a hole.',
  },
  {
    claim: 'OpenMetadata is one Postgres, which is what put it ahead of DataHub.',
    correction:
      'OpenMetadata requires Elasticsearch or OpenSearch, not optionally. It is **two stateful services**, not one — and that was the argument that put it ahead of DataHub.',
  },
  {
    claim: 'Marquez upgrades cheaply into the chosen catalog.',
    correction:
      'OpenMetadata’s OpenLineage ingest is a Kafka/Kinesis consumer, not an HTTP endpoint. So the cheap upgrade from Marquez is **DataHub** — a base-URL change — while OpenMetadata needs a Kafka hop. The reversal path out of ADR 0012 and the destination chosen in ADR 0013 therefore point at different products. Both records say so; it is a real tension, not an oversight.',
  },
];

const btnSecondary =
  'inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2 rounded-lg border border-line-strong px-5 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-fg transition-colors hover:bg-surface-2';
---

<Base
  title="Roadmap"
  description="The eleven gaps this reference architecture has not closed, each with a decision recorded as an ADR — and the one bar every external tool had to clear."
  active="roadmap"
>
  {/* ---------------------------- Header ---------------------------- */}
  <section class="border-b border-line">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 sm:py-24 lg:px-8">
      <p class="font-mono text-xs tracking-widest text-muted uppercase">Roadmap</p>
      <h1 class="mt-4 text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance sm:text-5xl">
        {tally.total} gaps, {tally.planned + tally.open} of them still open at the code level
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-pretty text-muted">
        This repository proves one half of an enterprise application well: who you are, what you may
        do, what happened, and whose data it is. It is close to silent on the other half — where data
        comes from, where it goes, how it is traced, how it is integrated, and how its contracts
        change. Each item below is a decision you can read and disagree with, not a promise.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-8">
        <Scoreboard variant="inline" />
      </div>

      <div class="mt-10 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a class={btnSecondary} href={withBase('/docs/roadmap/')}>The full roadmap, in prose</a>
        <a class={btnSecondary} href={withBase('/docs/adr/')}>All decision records</a>
        <a class={btnSecondary} href={withBase('/#scoreboard')}>The 28-question checklist</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ---------------------------- The bar ---------------------------- */}
  <section class="border-b border-line bg-surface-2/50">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 sm:py-20 lg:px-8">
      <div class="grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,1.15fr)] lg:items-start">
        <div>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance sm:text-3xl">
            The bar every item had to clear
          </h2>
          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted">
            There is exactly one selection rule, and it disqualified several tools that would
            otherwise have won on features.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <blockquote
            class="rounded-2xl border border-brand/30 bg-surface p-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-pretty shadow-card sm:p-8"
          >
            An external tool earns a place only if it can be handed a thin, generated declaration and
            delegate authorization back to{' '}
            <code class="rounded bg-code-bg px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[0.85em]"
              >AshEnterprise.Security.ActorContext</code
            >.
          </blockquote>
          <p class="mt-5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
            A tool that brings its own RBAC, its own tenancy model or its own audit trail is a second
            copy of the security model to keep synchronised. Every synchronisation job diverges
            eventually, and it diverges silently in the permissive direction. Three strong products
            lose on that specifically: Metabase, Camunda/Flowable, and Unleash.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ------------------- Premises that did not survive ------------------- */}
  <section class="border-b border-line">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 sm:py-20 lg:px-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance sm:text-3xl">
        Where the neat story did not survive checking
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed text-muted">
        Four premises this roadmap was drafted on turned out to be wrong, and correcting them is more
        useful than the tidier version.
      </p>

      <ol class="mt-10 space-y-6">
        {
          corrections.map(({ claim, correction }, i) => (
            <li class="grid gap-4 rounded-xl border border-line bg-surface p-6 sm:grid-cols-[auto_minmax(0,1fr)] sm:gap-6">
              <span
                class="grid h-8 w-8 shrink-0 place-items-center rounded-lg bg-surface-2 font-mono text-xs font-semibold text-muted"
                aria-hidden="true"
              >
                {i + 1}
              </span>
              <div>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted line-through decoration-line-strong">{claim}</p>
                <p
                  class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-pretty"
                  set:html={inlineMarkdown(correction).replace(
                    /\*\*([^*]+)\*\*/g,
                    '<strong class="font-semibold">$1</strong>',
                  )}
                />
              </div>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ---------------------------- The items ---------------------------- */}
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    {
      groups.map(({ priority, items: group }) => {
        const intro = priorityIntro[priority];
        return (
          <section id={`priority-${priority}`} class="scroll-mt-24 border-b border-line py-16 sm:py-20">
            <header class="max-w-3xl">
              <p class="flex items-center gap-3 font-mono text-xs tracking-widest text-muted uppercase">
                <span class="rounded border border-line px-1.5 py-0.5 text-brand">
                  Priority {priority}
                </span>
                {intro && <span>{intro.label}</span>}
                <span aria-hidden="true">·</span>
                <span>
                  {group.length} item{group.length === 1 ? '' : 's'}
                </span>
              </p>
              {intro && (
                <p
                  class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-pretty text-muted"
                  set:html={inlineMarkdown(intro.text)}
                />
              )}
            </header>

            <div class="mt-10 space-y-6">
              {group.map((item) => {
                const adr = adrHref(item.adr);
                return (
                  <article
                    id={item.id}
                    class="scroll-mt-24 rounded-2xl border border-line bg-surface p-6 transition-shadow hover:shadow-card sm:p-8"
                  >
                    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-start justify-between gap-x-6 gap-y-3">
                      <div class="min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance">
                          {item.title}
                        </h3>
                        <p class="mt-1.5 font-mono text-sm text-brand">{item.choice}</p>
                      </div>
                      <div class="flex shrink-0 items-center gap-3">
                        <StatusChip status={item.status} />
                        {adr ? (
                          <a
                            class="font-mono text-xs text-muted underline-offset-4 transition-colors hover:text-brand hover:underline"
                            href={adr}
                          >
                            ADR {item.adr}
                          </a>
                        ) : item.adr ? (
                          <span class="font-mono text-xs text-muted">ADR {item.adr}</span>
                        ) : null}
                      </div>
                    </div>

                    <dl class="mt-7 grid gap-7 sm:grid-cols-2">
                      <div>
                        <dt class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-widest text-muted uppercase">
                          What the work actually is
                        </dt>
                        <dd
                          class="mt-2.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-pretty"
                          set:html={inlineMarkdown(item.effort)}
                        />
                      </div>
                      {item.bar && (
                        <div>
                          <dt class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-widest text-muted uppercase">
                            Does it consume ActorContext?
                          </dt>
                          <dd
                            class="mt-2.5 border-l-2 border-brand/40 pl-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-pretty text-muted"
                            set:html={inlineMarkdown(item.bar)}
                          />
                        </div>
                      )}
                    </dl>
                  </article>
                );
              })}
            </div>
          </section>
        );
      })
    }
  </div>

  {/* ---------------------------- Closing ---------------------------- */}
  <section class="py-16 sm:py-24">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 text-center sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance sm:text-3xl">
        How this page stays honest
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-pretty text-muted">
        Every status word above is read at build time from <code
          class="rounded bg-code-bg px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[0.9em]">docs/roadmap.json</code
        >, the same file the README, the checklist and the prose roadmap render from, and checked in
        CI alongside <code class="rounded bg-code-bg px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[0.9em]"
          >mix ash.codegen --check</code
        >. A status can be wrong in one place only by being wrong in all of them — which is easier to
        notice.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-9 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-3">
        <a class={btnSecondary} href={withBase('/docs/roadmap/')}>The full roadmap</a>
        <a class={btnSecondary} href={withBase('/docs/manifesto/07-what-we-do-not-have/')}>
          What we do not have
        </a>
        <a class={btnSecondary} href={withBase('/')}>Back to the overview</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
